Before: a list of expandable rows keyed by index. Deleting the first row causes the *second* row to appear expanded, even though the user never touched it:

```jsx
// Bug: index as key
{comments.map((c, i) => <CommentRow key={i} comment={c} />)}

// Fix: stable id as key
{comments.map((c) => <CommentRow key={c.id} comment={c} />)}
```

With `key={i}`, deleting `comments[0]` shifts every remaining comment down one index. React sees "the component at key `0` is still there, just with new props" — so it reuses the *first* `CommentRow` instance, including its internal `isExpanded` state, for what is now a completely different comment. With `key={c.id}`, the identity travels with the data: deleting a comment removes exactly the `CommentRow` instance keyed to that id, and every other row's instance — and its internal state — is untouched, because the framework can see they're the same items, just fewer of them.
